<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<package xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/packaging/2015/06/nuspec.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <id>prtop</id>
    <version>{version}</version>
    <title>prtop</title>
    <authors>Joris La Cancellera</authors>
    <owners>TomyCesaille</owners>
    <projectUrl>https://github.com/TomyCesaille/prtop</projectUrl>
    <projectSourceUrl>https://github.com/TomyCesaille/prtop</projectSourceUrl>
    <packageSourceUrl>https://github.com/TomyCesaille/prtop/tree/main/packaging/chocolatey</packageSourceUrl>
    <docsUrl>https://github.com/TomyCesaille/prtop#readme</docsUrl>
    <bugTrackerUrl>https://github.com/TomyCesaille/prtop/issues</bugTrackerUrl>
    <licenseUrl>https://github.com/TomyCesaille/prtop/blob/main/LICENSE</licenseUrl>
    <requireLicenseAcceptance>false</requireLicenseAcceptance>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/TomyCesaille/prtop/blob/v{version}/CHANGELOG.md</releaseNotes>
    <tags>gitlab github merge-requests pull-requests code-review terminal cli htop</tags>
    <summary>Your merge requests and pull requests, from review to production, in one terminal table that refreshes in place, like htop.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[prtop shows your GitLab merge requests and GitHub pull requests in one terminal table that refreshes in place, the way htop shows processes. Each row is marked with whose move it is: `!` on you now, `?` on you to look at, `~` on someone else, `.` on a machine. Once a merge request merges, prtop follows its pipeline to production and drops the row when production runs it.

prtop reads GitLab through the `glab` CLI and GitHub through the `gh` CLI, which this package installs, and never writes to either. Sign in to the ones you use, tell prtop what to read, and run it:

```
glab auth login --hostname gitlab.example.com
gh auth login
prtop
```

Settings live in `%APPDATA%\prtop\.env`; the README lists them.

Run prtop in Windows Terminal, the default terminal on Windows 11: the older console shows the emoji as boxes and cannot open links.

The package carries its own Python {python_version}, from python.org, kept off the PATH: only the `prtop` command is added.
]]></description>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency id="glab" />
      <dependency id="gh" />
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>
  <files>
    <file src="tools\**" target="tools" />
    <file src="legal\**" target="legal" />
  </files>
</package>
